###### Abstract

We report the discovery of a new X-ray pulsar, XTE J1810-197. The source was serendipitously discovered on 2003 July 15 by the _Rossi X-ray Timing Explorer_ (_RXTE_) while observing the soft gamma repeater SGR 1806-20. The pulsar has a 5.54 s spin-period and a soft spectrum (photon index \\approx 4). We detect the source in earlier RXTE observations back to 2003 January. These show that a transient outburst began between 2002 November 17 and 2003 January 23 and that the pulsar has been spinning down since then, with a high rate \\dot{P}\\approx 10^{-11} s s{}^{-1} showing significant timing noise, but no evidence for Doppler shifts due to a binary companion. The rapid spin-down rate and slow spin-period imply a super-critical magnetic field B=3\\times 10^{14} G and a young characteristic age \\tau\\leq 7600 yr. These properties are strikingly similar to those of anomalous X-ray pulsars and soft gamma repeaters, making the source a likely new magnetar. A follow-up _Chandra_ observation provided a 2\\farcs 5 radius error circle within which the 1.5 m _Russian-Turkish_ Optical Telescope _RTT150_ found a limiting magnitude of R_{c}=21.5, in accord with other recently reported limits. The source is present in archival _ASCA_ and _ROSAT_ data as well, at a level 100 times fainter than the \\approx 3 mCrab seen in 2003. This suggests that other X-ray sources that are currently in a state similar to the inactive phase of XTE J1810-197 may also be unidentified magnetars awaiting detection via a similar activity.

